How SOMO builds it
------------------

src/us_zeno_lib.cpp compiles the library as one translation unit with the settings
in us_somo_zeno_config.h:

  - no MPI, even in the MPI programs (us_saxs_cmds_mpi), which define USE_MPI
  - the LeapFrog random number generator, NIST ZENO's choice when SPRNG is not
    available (SPRNG is not bundled)
  - no voxel (.fits.gz) input, which would need zlib

It compiles libzeno/src/{Parameters*,Results*,Timer,Units,Zeno}.cc,
libzeno/src/RandomNumber/LeapFrog.cc and zeno-cli/src/{ParametersLocal.cc,cmdline.c}.
libzeno/src/RandomNumber/{Rand,SPRNG}.cc are kept only to mirror NIST's tree.

src/us_zeno_cxx.cpp is SOMO's driver (zeno_cxx_main()), adapted from NIST's
zeno-cli/src/Main.cc.  Its header comment lists how it differs from Main.cc.  It
reads .bod files with its own parser, so Boost (needed by NIST's parser) is not
used.


Changes to NIST's files
-----------------------

Each change is marked "US-SOMO" in the source.

1. libzeno/include/Geometry/MixedModel.h: addTriangle(), so that .bod files can
   give triangles (TRIANGLE x1 y1 z1 x2 y2 z2 x3 y3 z3).  NIST ZENO 5.3.1 has the
   triangle geometry, nearest-point search and inside/outside test, but no way to
   add triangles to a model.  The walks (capacitance and polarizability, hence
   Rh, D and [eta]) only need the surface.  The interior sampling (volume, Rg)
   decides inside from the nearest triangle, so the mesh must be closed and wound
   counterclockwise seen from outside.

2. libzeno/include/Zeno.h, libzeno/src/Zeno.cc: a polling hook.  An optional
   callback runs on the calling thread about every 200 ms while walks or interior
   samples run.  It gets the number done and can ask to stop.  SOMO uses it for
   its progress bar, UDP progress messages and Stop button.  Without a callback,
   ZENO works as before.

3. libzeno/include/Geometry/Voxels.h: with ZENO_NO_VOXELS defined, zlib is not
   needed and voxel files are rejected.

4. libzeno/src/Units.cc: Avogadro's number and Boltzmann's constant are the
   CODATA 2014 values in SOMO's ZENO 5.0.1 copy (6.022140857e23, 1.38064852e-23)
   rather than NIST's 6.02214e23 and 1.38065e-23.


Updating to a newer NIST ZENO
-----------------------------

Copy the same files from the new release over these, re-apply the changes above
(git log and git diff on this directory show them), and compare SOMO's ZENO
results on bead models before and after.
